es who gets the most  votes without necessarily being a  majority they will by default become a  majority and you could imagine why the  fascists wanted this to happen they felt  that they could get 25 percent of the  votes popular won maybe through popular  support but also with the help of the  help of the coercive tactics of the  Blackshirts and then that would give  them stronger stronger control in in in  the legislature now the big question is  is why would the legislature pass this  because at this point the fascists were  not the dominant party they did not have  a majority in the legislature in fact  this is why they wanted to pass the law  because they didn't have a majority and  once again it's a one of those questions  of history some would say that there was  actually people were enamored with the  fascist they were enamored with  Mussolini they were eager to have strong  leadership they didn't want this  government of coalition's they wanted  one government to be able to take action  and on the other side when the votes  were happening you actually had black  shirts in the room so one argument is  that there was also an element of pure  intimidation but needless to say the  chair of all law or share Bowl law  actually passed but there's an irony  here because it was unnecessary in 1924  when you actually have elections you  have the fascists getting 2/3 of the  vote fascists get 2/3 of the vote now  many in today and many in Italy at the  time felt that this was a fraudulent  election they felt that they were the  reason why the fascist were able to get  so many votes is that because they were  able to intimidate folks they were able  to commit fraud during the election that  they were able to kind of throw other  votes out and one of the most outspoken  individuals when it came to criticizing  the fascists and and their their tactics  of coming to power was Giacomo Matteotti  and he wrote a book about the fascists  he gave two really strong speeches in in  in  in the in the deputy of ministers where  he talks about where he ordered the  Chamber of Deputies I should say where  he he talks about the the kind of the  corruption and the and the the the  violence of the fascist a few days after  giving those speeches he gets killed by  black shirts so he gets he gets actually  quite violently murdered murdered by  black shirts and this puts this puts  Mussolini in at least initially a bit of  a bind  he he he he doesn't want to look like a  thug someone who goes out and and you  just just murders people it's not clear  that he actually Mussolini was involved  in this in any way but his followers had  committed this act and to protest  against the murder of Giacomo Matteotti  you actually have the entire Socialist  Party boycotts Parliament and this is  known as the Aventine secession or at  least a twentieth-century Aventine  secession Aventine secession is called  the Aventine secession because if you go  back to Roman times 2500 years ago you  had the plebeians secede out of protest  from from harsh rule and they go to the  Aventine hill and so it was named after  that same idea and the whole reason why  the the Socialists did this is they  hoped that by by by boycotting  Parliament that that would convince the  king to to to get rid of to get rid of  Benito Mussolini and Mussolini at the  same is also in a bind he doesn't know  quite what to do and on top of all of  this the black shirts are telling him  look if you don't take control of the  situation if you don't become a strong  ruler we're gonna do it without you we  we might even overthrow you mr.  Mussolini and so in 1925 early 1925  Mussolini makes his famous January  speech 1925 is famous January speech and  this is normally viewed as the formal  start of his of his absolute  dictatorship in this Mussolini instead  of the the instead of the Aventine  secession somehow making undermining  Mussolini's power because the king did  not dismiss Mussolini it actually  strengthened Mussolini  power he used that as a pretext he said  look all of these deputies they've  decide not to show up at Parliament  they've essentially given up their seats  and he bans he bans the Italian  Socialist Party he embraces the black  shirts he takes responsibility for them  he doesn't take responsibility directly  for Giacomo's Giacomo Matteotti murder  but he takes responsibility for the  black shirts and he gives in kind of  classic Mussolini style a somewhat  convoluted argument about how strength  how strength and violence is going to  give stability to the Italian people  obviously he's a he's a it's an amazing  orator he's very charismatic and this  essentially gives him the control he  needs and by the end of 1925 you have  the Christmas Eve law that's passed by  by Parliament that essentially puts no  checks on Mussolini's power and as you  go then into 1926 they more and more the  fascists under Mussolini take absolute  control absolute power of Italy banning  banning so 19 1926 they're banning other  parties so other other parties other  parties are banned they're starting to  force people to become members of the  fascist party if they want roles in the  government or even in any type of  institution they're trying to take  control of the press they're starting to  have a a very strong state police  architecture and if this looks familiar  based on what we studied about the Nazis  it's it's it's not a coincidence  Hitler was a he looked he admired  Mussolini in fact Mussolini's march on  Rome inspired Hitler to attempt his Beer  Hall pooched in 1922 or in 1923 a lot of  these tactics that brought Mussolini to  power you see it kind of a parallel in  what brought Hitler to power only about  seven years later
 

